Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 10   Projective Geometry\n",
  StyleBox["Duality and Conics",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["21 October 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subtitle",
  FontSize->18],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Curves and Dual Curves", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Parameterization", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Parameterizing a conic",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Subsubsection",
  FontWeight->"Plain"],

Cell["\<\
We begin by proving a general theorem about intersecting lines with \
a curve.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " If ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a curve of degree ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["(n-1)-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "tuple point on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is any point ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the tangents at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we can find the ",
  StyleBox["other intersection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the line ",
  StyleBox["PE[p,q]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " with the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by the formula \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, q\^n]\ p - n\ PD[f, p\ q\^\(n - 1\)]\ q\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "(by example)",
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We use ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  to do a ",
  StyleBox["symbolic proof",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for the case of degree ",
  StyleBox["5",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  First declare ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be a curve of this degree:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f]; \)\), 
    \(\(f /: DegP[f] = 5; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now clear information about ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["q",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and declare them to be points:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[p, q]; \)\), 
    \(\(p /: DegP[p] = 1; \)\), 
    \(\(q /: DegP[q] = 1; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We use the formula (for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n == 5\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to introduce the desired point on the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p, q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(r = PD[f, q\^5]\ p - 5\ PD[f, p\ q\^4]\ q; \)\), 
    \(DegP[r]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now we try to check the theorem by seeing if ",
  StyleBox["r",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " lies on the curve:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(check = Expand[PD[f, r\^5]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "At first this does not look too hopeful, but we see that so far ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  does not know all the necessary facts about the curve.  In particular, \
we have not yet told it that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["(n-1)-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "tuple point.  Here is the necessary declaration:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(p /: PD[PD[f, p], p] = 0; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So let's look at the value of ",
  StyleBox["check",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " again, as ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " will keep simplifying the expression as we enter more information:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(check\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Good!  The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["does",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " lie on the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Clearly if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is not on any of the tangents, then, because the scalar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\ q\^4]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "does not vanish, the linear combination defining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " gives an answer different from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".  Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["The formula of the theorem has a use for parameterization.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If we choose a convenient parameterization for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (say, on a line ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " passing through ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "), then the formula of the theorem gives us a (polynomial) ",
  StyleBox["parameterization",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the points on the curve."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "In case ",
  StyleBox["q",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["in fact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "on one of the tangents, then the formula gives an answer equivalent to ",
  StyleBox["p.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The formula thus always gives ",
  StyleBox["some",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " point on the curve, and clearly ",
  StyleBox["every other",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point can be so represented by passing a line through ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " the point on the curve and finding where it intersects the line being \
used for parameters.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The formula of the theorem also has a very useful consequence for ",
  StyleBox["conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a point on the conic.  If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a point ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "on the curve, then the line through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " intersects the conic in a third point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and intersects the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, \ q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " so that the one pair ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, \ r}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " separates the other pair ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({q, \ s}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["harmonically",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Proof:",
  StyleBox[
  "  We can set up the whole proof by symbolic computation.  Here are the \
declarations for ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", and the definitions of ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, p, q, r, s]; \)\), 
    \(\(f /: DegP[f] = 2; \)\), 
    \(\(p /: DegP[p] = 1; \)\), 
    \(\(q /: DegP[q] = 1; \)\), 
    \(\(p /: PD[PD[f, p], p] = 0; \)\), 
    \(\(r = PD[f, q\^2]\ p - 2\ PD[f, p\ q]\ q; \)\), 
    \(\(s = PD[f, q\^2]\ p - PD[f, p\ q]\ q; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["First, the new points are indeed points.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(DegP[r] == DegP[s] == 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As in the previous theorem, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\[LongDash]while by construction it lies on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p, \ q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, r\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we check the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\[LongDash] while we know that ",
  StyleBox["by construction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " it lies on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[p, q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[PD[f, q], s]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since all the points are linear combinations of ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["q",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we can use the standard procedure to check ",
  StyleBox["crossratios",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(RP[p, r, q, s]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It works, so we have a ",
  StyleBox["harmonic tetrad",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Here is a picture, using the calculations in the above proof, where we \
have put vertical lines to mark ",
  StyleBox["q",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["s",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["r",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in that order.  The vertical line at ",
  StyleBox["s",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is in fact the line ",
  StyleBox["PD[f, q]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f = x\^2\/4 + y\^2 - z\^2; \)\), 
    \(\(p = \(-2\)\ u + w; \)\), 
    \(\(q = \(-3.5\)\ u + w; \)\), 
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, y == 0, PE[q, v] == 0, PD[f, p] == 0, 
          PD[f, q] == 0, PD[f, r] == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, \(-4\), 4}, {y, 
        \(-2\), 2}, Axes \[Rule] None, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Let us take ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as the ",
  StyleBox["unit circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the unit point on the ",
  StyleBox["x-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "axis; we let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " run up and down the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, p, q, r]\), 
    \(f = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \np = w + u; \nq = b\ w + a\ v; \n
    r = Tidy[1\/2\ PD[f, q\^2]\ p - PD[f, p\ q]\ q]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The above result thus gives a nice ",
  StyleBox["parameterization",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of points on the conic.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[PD[f, r\^2]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Consider the line through ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " that determines this intersection:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[p, q]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now consider the line in the pencil centered at ",
  StyleBox["w + v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " that also passes through this point:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Factor[PE[r, w + v]]\), 
    \(Tidy[Last[%]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This shows that there is a ",
  StyleBox["linear transformation ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "determined here from the lines in the ",
  StyleBox["first pencil",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the corresponding lines in the ",
  StyleBox["second pencil",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  That means ",
  StyleBox["crossratios are preserved",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This is in fact a general property of conics.  Here is a picture."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, p, q, r]; \((f = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; )\)\), 
    \(\(p = w + u; \)\), 
    \(\(q[t_] := w + t\ v; \)\), 
    \(r[t_] := PD[f, q[t]\^2]\ p - 2\ PD[f, p\ q[t]]\ q[t]; \n\n
    ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{f == 0}, Table[PE[p, q[t]] == 0, {t, \(-5\), \(-1\)}], \n\t\t\ \ 
          Table[PE[w + v, r[t]] == 0, {t, \(-5\), \(-1\)}]] /. {
          z \[Rule] 1}, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, Axes \[Rule] None]; 
    \)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Parameterizing a cubic", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
We take the example of a cubic with a double point at the origin:\
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f]; f = x\^3 + y\^3 + x\ y\ z; \), 
    \(p = w; q = a\ u + b\ v; \)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      \((f /. z \[Rule] 1)\) == 0, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, 
      Axes \[Rule] False, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We check that ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a double point:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, p\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here are the tangents at the origin:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, p]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{\((f /. z \[Rule] 1)\) == 0, x == 0, y == 0}, {x, 
        \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now we use the formula to represent an arbitrary point on the \
curve:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Tidy[1\/6\ \((PD[f, q\^3]\ p - 3\ PD[f, p\ q\^2]\ q)\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If we assume ",
  StyleBox["b \[NotEqual] 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we can divide through by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(b\^3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and set ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c = a\/b\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and we have:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(pt[c_] := \(-c\^2\)\ u - c\ v + \((c\^3 + 1)\)\ w; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["We check the parameterized point lies on the curve.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[PD[f, pt[c]\^3]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Thus, the finite points on the cubic can be parameterized as:",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({\(-\(c\^2\/\(1 + c\^3\)\)\), \(-\(c\/\(1 + c\^3\)\)\)}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In this parameterization there is a singularity at ",
  StyleBox["c == -1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and so we make a change of variable to send this value to infinity:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Together[{\(-\(c\^2\/\(1 + c\^3\)\)\), \(-\(c\/\(1 + c\^3\)\)\)} /. 
        c \[Rule] \(-1\) + 1\/d]\)], "Input"],

Cell["There are no real roots of the denominator:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Denominator[%\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[% == 0, {d}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["We can now plot the curve over a central range:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot[{
        \(-\(\(\((1 - d)\)\^2\ d\)\/\(1 - 3\ d + 3\ d\^2\)\)\), 
        \(-\(\(\((1 - d)\)\ d\^2\)\/\(1 - 3\ d + 3\ d\^2\)\)\)}, {d, \(-5\), 
        5}, AspectRatio \[Rule] 1, 
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-2\), 2}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The vertical and horizontal tangents at the double point are again clearly \
seen.  The point at infinity ",
  StyleBox["u-v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " obviously lies on the curve.  Here is the tangent at infinity:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, \((u - v)\)\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The line ",
  StyleBox["x + y = 1/3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " will thus appear as an ",
  StyleBox["asymptote",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{\((f /. z \[Rule] 1)\) == 0, x == 0, y == 0, 
        x + y == 1\/3}, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["We can investigate further what is happening at infinity.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(q = u - v; r = u\/3 + w; \), 
    \(PE[q, r]\), 
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{q\^3, q\^2\ r, q\ r\^2, r\^3}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The asymptote is thus seen to pass through an ",
  StyleBox["inflection point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at infinity (namely, at ",
  StyleBox["u - v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ").  In order to see infinity, we interchange ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and leave ",
  StyleBox["v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " fixed.  (This is the same as interchanging ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".)  The inflection point then is shown at ",
  StyleBox["w - v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{\((f /. {x \[Rule] 1, z \[Rule] x})\) == 0, 
        \((3\ x + 3\ y - z /. {x \[Rule] 1, z \[Rule] x})\) == 0, x == 0, 
        y == 0}, {x, \(-10\), 10}, {y, \(-10\), 10}, Axes \[Rule] False, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The tangential equation of a curve", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A general procedure", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
From what we already know about tangents, we can derive at once the \
truth of the following theorem.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proposition.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Let  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the (point) equation of a curve of degree ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " .        Let ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(t == u\ x + v\ y + w\ z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " be a general line. Assume further that  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p == a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["simple point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the curve. Then the following two equations are the conditions for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["t",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the curve at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":\n\t\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^\(n - 1\)] == t\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(PD[t, p] == 0\ \)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By equating coefficients in the first equation, we obtain altogether ",
  StyleBox["4",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " equations in variables ",
  StyleBox[" a, b, c, u,v,w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". We can then ",
  StyleBox["eliminate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["a, b,",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["c",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", to get a single equation in ",
  StyleBox["u,v,",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". This is the ",
  StyleBox["line equation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the ",
  StyleBox["envelope",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of all the tangents to the curve.  Here is the implementation being \
used."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["?Envel", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "WARNING:  ",
  StyleBox["We have not actually  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["proved",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[
  " that the elimination procedure \[LongDash] in the case of a \
non-degererate curve \[LongDash] results in a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["single equation",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[
  "for the dual curve representing the envelope.  This takes some heavy \
algebra to do so, and we cannot take the time to develop the techniques.  But \
we can at least ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["test",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" the results.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We try first a ",
  StyleBox["special conic ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "and find by this method the polynomial representing the ",
  StyleBox["dual",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, a, b, c, d, e, r, s, t]; \)\), 
    \(f = a\ x\^2 + b\ x\ y + c\ y\^2 + d\ z\^2; \ne = Envel[f]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
What is the condition to be a non-degenerate conic?  The Hessian \
determinant must not vanish.  Here it is.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Det[MHP[f]]//Factor", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Thus, we must have ",
  StyleBox["d \[NotEqual] 0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(b\^2 - 4\ a\ c \[NotEqual] 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Now, if a point ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\ u + s\ v + t\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then linear form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, r\ u + s\ v + t\ w]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is the ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the point, and this line should lie on the ",
  StyleBox["dual curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Here is a computation to check this."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[PD[f, r\ u + s\ v + t\ w]\^2, e]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Yes!  we see that this quantity vanishes ",
  StyleBox["if, and only if,",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the given point lies on the curve.  This example shows that all tangents \
",
  StyleBox["do",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " lie on the dual curve.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Conversely, what about ",
  StyleBox["other possible lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the dual?  Well, let the linear form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(a\_1\ x\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(b\_1\ y\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(c\_1\ z\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " be such a line.  Then the ",
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[a\_1\ x + b\_1\ y + c\_1\ z, e]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(a\_1\ x\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(b\_1\ y\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(c\_1\ z\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ".  But it also lies on the curve by this calculation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{"Factor", "[", 
      RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
        RowBox[{"f", ",", 
          SuperscriptBox[
            RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox[\(a\_1\ x + b\_1\ y + c\_1\ z\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], ",", "e"}], "]"}], "2"]}], "]"}], 
      "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell["And the tangent at that point is the expected line.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{"Factor", "[", 
      RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
        RowBox[{"f", ",", 
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox[\(a\_1\ x\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["+",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox[\(b\_1\ y\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["+",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox[\(c\_1\ z\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], ",", "e"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So we have now checked that the dual consits ",
  StyleBox["exactly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of all the tangents to the original conic."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We look next at the completely general conic. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, b, c, d, e, f]; \)\), 
    \(conic = {a, 2\ b, c, 2\ d, 2\ e, f} . Bxyz[2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dual = \((Factor/@#1&)\)[Envel[conic]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
And we make the check about the relationship of the conic to its \
dual.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[PD[conic, r\ u + s\ v + t\ w]\^2, dual]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[conic, PD[uu\ x + vv\ y + ww\ z, dual]\^2]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[conic, PD[uu\ x + vv\ y + ww\ z, dual]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["And it works.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Unfortunately, there can be degenerate curves, and for them the finding of \
the envelope ",
  StyleBox["cannot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " use the general algebraic method of ",
  StyleBox["Eliminate",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 3.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Here is an example of a ",
  StyleBox["degenerate conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (where by fiat the implementation gives ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["Fail",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as an answer)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, b, c, d, e, f]; \)\), 
    \(prod = Tidy[\((a\ x + b\ y + c\ z)\)\ \((d\ x + e\ y + f\ z)\)]\n\), 
    \(Envel[prod]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We can see why there is trouble by going back to the general \
case.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(conic = {a, b, c, d, e, f} . Bxyz[2]\), 
    \(dual = \((Factor/@#1&)\)[Envel[conic]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The formula for the dual looks very good, but what happens when the \
coefficients for the degenerate product are substituted?\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[Tidy, 
      Factor[dual /. 
          Thread[{a, b, c, d, e, f} \[Rule] Coefficient[prod, Bxyz[2]]]], {2}]
      \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The answer is a ",
  StyleBox["square",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and indeed it is the square of the point at the ",
  StyleBox["intersection ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of the two lines making up the degenerate conic.  In otherwords the dual \
is a",
  StyleBox[" double pencil of lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Tidy[PE[a\ x + b\ y + c\ z, d\ x + e\ y + f\ z]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But, if the original conic is a ",
  StyleBox["square",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then the answer completely degererates to ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(% /. {d \[Rule] a, e \[Rule] b, f \[Rule] c}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In both these cases we prefer to let the ",
  StyleBox["Envel",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " program simple to fail.  When this happens, the specific case can be \
analyzed to see what is the matter."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Two cubics", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Here is a cubic with a ",
  StyleBox["4",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th degree dual curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[cubic]; \)\), 
    \(\(cubic = 3\ x\ y\ z - x\^3 + y\^3; \)\), 
    \(dual = Envel[cubic]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["\<\
This is the picture of the central part of the (real part of the) \
curve.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[3\ x\ y == x\^3 - y\^3, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}]; \)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell["We can use our general method to parameterize the curve.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[ct]; \)\), 
    \(\(ct[t_] := 
      Tidy[1\/6\ 
          \((PD[cubic, \((u + t\ v + w)\)\^3]\ w - 
              3\ PD[cubic, w\ \((u + t\ v + w)\)\^2]\ \((u + t\ v + w)\))\)]; 
    \)\), 
    \(ct[t]\), 
    \(Expand[PD[cubic, ct[t]\^3]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Here is a typical tangent to the curve.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Expand[PD[cubic, ct[1\/2]\^2]]\), 
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{\((cubic /. z \[Rule] 1)\) == 0, 
        \((PD[cubic, ct[\(-\(1\/2\)\)]\^2] /. z \[Rule] 1)\) == 0}, {x, 
        \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell["We can check that all tangents lie on the dual curve.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[PD[PD[cubic, ct[t]\^2]\^4, dual]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Finally, we can generate sequences of tangents to the curve, \
showing how the tangents form an envelope.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{cubic == 0}, 
          Table[PD[cubic, ct[t]\^2] == 0, {t, \(-6\), \(-2\), 0.5}]] /. {
          z \[Rule] 1}, {x, \(-5\), 5}, {y, \(-5\), 5}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{cubic == 0}, Table[PD[cubic, ct[t]\^2] == 0, {t, 1, 6}]] /. {
          z \[Rule] 1}, {x, \(-5\), 5}, {y, \(-5\), 5}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Calculation showed that \[LongDash] generally speaking \[LongDash] from a \
point outside this curve there were two real tangents and two complex \
tangents corresponding to the fact that the dual curve has degree ",
  StyleBox["4",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Here next is a curve without a double point and with a ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree dual (which is the maximum degree the dual of a cubic can \
have)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(cubic = x\ y\^2 + y\ z\^2 - z\ x\^2; \ndual = Expand[Envel[cubic]]\)], 
  "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      \((cubic /. z \[Rule] 1)\) == 0, {x, \(-5\), 5}, {y, \(-5\), 5}, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As for tangents, here is a case where there are three real tangents \
\[LongDash] all apparently ",
  StyleBox["double",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = 
      NSolve[{\((dual /. w \[Rule] 1)\) == 0, \(-u\) - v + 1 == 0}, {u, v}]
        \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{\((cubic /. z \[Rule] 1)\) == 0}, u\ x + v\ y + 1 == 0 /. sol], {
        x, \(-5\), 5}, {y, \(-5\), 5}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And here is a position giving  ",
  StyleBox["four ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "real tangents and ",
  StyleBox["one double",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " complex tangent."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = 
      NSolve[{\((dual /. w \[Rule] 1)\) == 0, \(-5\)\ u - 2\ v + 1 == 0}, {u, 
          v}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{\((cubic /. z \[Rule] 1)\) == 0}, 
        u\ x + v\ y + 1 == 0 /. 
          sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]{1, 2, 3, 6}\[RightDoubleBracket]], {x, 
        \(-5\), 20}, {y, \(-5\), 5}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Specifying Conics ", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Specifying conics and dual conics", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Finding the conic through five points", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "How many conics are there?  Well, of course, there are infinitely many \
conics, but maybe they can be organized in some way.  As we know, the ",
  StyleBox["projective dimension",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the space of quadratic polynomials in three variables is  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(5\ \(( == 6 - 1)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This suggests that we think of the conics as forming a projective \
space.  From the calculation of demension, this ought to mean that it takes",
  StyleBox[" five points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to determine a conic.  That is to say, there are only a few conics \
satisfying given conditions \[LongDash] the more conditions the fewer the \
conics.  We will give several examples."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Indeed, suppose that ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a quadratic ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomial.  As we have discussed before, the space of quadratic ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials (of linear dimension  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["6",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", because there are six ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-monomials) is ",
  StyleBox["dual",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the space of polynomials in the other variables, and the operation of \
polydifferentiation gives the  ",
  StyleBox["dual paring ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " between these spaces.  Thus, supposing that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is not ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then the subspace of all ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, g] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is of linear dimension exactly  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["5",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  We now investigate this question of determination in more detail \
algebraically.  First, there are two theorems we have already discussed more \
generally."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Any set of  ",
  StyleBox["five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points, no three of which are collinear, determine a ",
  StyleBox["unique",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " non-degenerate conic.  Conversely, ",
  StyleBox["any five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " distinct points on a non-degenerate conic uniquely determine it. "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Distinct conics sharing no common linear factors can intersect in at \
most  ",
  StyleBox["four",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["\<\
Suppose that the five points are given by the following \
coordinates:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, Subscript]; \n
    MatrixForm[{{a\_1, b\_1, c\_1}, {a\_2, b\_2, c\_2}, {a\_3, b\_3, c\_3}, {
          a\_4, b\_4, c\_4}, {a\_5, b\_5, c\_5}}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The problem is to ",
  StyleBox["find",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the actual polynomial representing the conic passing through these \
points.  It is not hard to see that to write out one such polynomial, what we \
need to do is to multiply out the determinant of the following matrix:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(mm = {Bxyz[2], \n\t\t
        Bxyz[2] /. {x \[Rule] a\_1, y \[Rule] b\_1, z \[Rule] c\_1}, 
        Bxyz[2] /. {x \[Rule] a\_2, y \[Rule] b\_2, z \[Rule] c\_2}, 
        Bxyz[2] /. {x \[Rule] a\_3, y \[Rule] b\_3, z \[Rule] c\_3}, 
        Bxyz[2] /. {x \[Rule] a\_4, y \[Rule] b\_4, z \[Rule] c\_4}, 
        Bxyz[2] /. {x \[Rule] a\_5, y \[Rule] b\_5, z \[Rule] c\_5}}; \n
    MatrixForm[mm]\), 
    \(Tidy[Det[mm]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This curve passes through the fivr given points, because, if we put the \
coordinates of any one of the five points in for the  ",
  StyleBox["variables",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the determinant obviously vanishes.  Since it is a  ",
  StyleBox["quadratic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial, it must be the (?) answer.  Of course, it is not a formula to \
be memorized!"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " How do we know that this polynomial is ",
  StyleBox["non-zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "? "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Answer:",
  StyleBox[
  "  The non-triviality of the determinant follows from the fact that the \
five forms",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\((a\_i\ u + b\_i\ v + c\_i\ w)\)\^2\)],
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[",where",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(i == 1,  ... \ , 5\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", are ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "linearly independent quadratic polynomials",
  StyleBox[".  (Because they are ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[
  "-independent, since no three of the points are collinear.)  Look at their \
coefficients:",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((a\_i\ u + b\_i\ v + c\_i\ w)\)\^2 // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Thus, the ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(5\[Times]6\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" matrix of coefficients of these  has rank ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["5",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["(meaning ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "not all",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(5\[Times]5\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" subdeterminants are ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["0 ",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["\[LongDash] the factor of ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["2",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["in some columns makes no difference to the determinants).  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "Therefore",
  StyleBox[", not all of the coefficients of the monster polynomial can be ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["0",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontSlant->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In the implementation, we have programmed this process both for ",
  StyleBox["polynomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["dual polynomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We call the two procedures ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["P2P",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["P2L",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "In invoking ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["P2P",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", say, with five points, remember that if ",
  StyleBox["four",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the five points are collinear, the procedure gives ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "; if only ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the five points are collinear, it gives ",
  StyleBox["the product of the two lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Here is a first example:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(conic = Tidy[P2P[u, v, w, u + v + w, u - v + 2\ w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us draw a picture with the ",
  StyleBox["tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the five points drawn in."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{conic == 0, \n\t\tPD[conic, u] == 0, \n\t\t
          PD[conic, v] == 0, \n\t\tPD[conic, w] == 0, \n\t\t
          PD[conic, u + v + w] == 0, \n\t\tPD[conic, u - v + 2\ w] == 0} /. 
        z \[Rule] 1, \n\t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\t
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.005], \ Red}, Blue, Blue, Blue, Blue, Blue}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that we can use the ",
  StyleBox["dual program",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to find the ",
  StyleBox["dual conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " satisfied by the five tangents.  It proves to be the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as the envelope \[LongDash] whose polynomial was found by a completely \
different algebraic method.  This exactly what was intended."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Factor[
      P2L@@\(\((PD[conic, #1]&)\)/@{u, v, w, u + v + w, u - v + 2\ w}\)]\n
    \), 
    \(Envel[conic]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, if we apply the method of envelopes ",
  StyleBox["twice",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] being careful to employ dual variables \[LongDash] we get the \
",
  StyleBox["original curve ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "back again."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Dual[Envel[Dual[Envel[conic]]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Here next is an example of a conic through given points and through a ",
  StyleBox["symbolic point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Tidy[P2P[w, u + w, v + w, u + v + w, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we see we get very simple conditions for this conic to be ",
  StyleBox["degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Det[MHP[%]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 3.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Next, to get the ",
  StyleBox["unit circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the conic has to pass through the ",
  StyleBox["circular points at infinity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "But the unit circle can, of course, also be determined by five real points \
as well."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Tidy[
      \(-\(P2P[\(-u\) + w, u + w, v + w, u + I\ v, u - I\ v]\/\(4\ I\)\)\)]\n
    \), 
    \(Tidy[
      \(-\(P2P[\(-u\) + w, u + w, \(-v\) + w, v + w, u - I\ v]\/\(4\ I\)\)\)]
      \n\), 
    \(Tidy[
      1\/8\ P2P[\(-u\) + w, u + w, \(-v\) + w, v + w, \@2\ u + \@2\ v + 2\ w]]
      \)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 4.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If we take three symbolic points and the ",
  StyleBox["two circular points at infinity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we get the arbitrary conic passing through them:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Tidy[
      \(-\(1\/\(2\ I\)\)\) 
        \((P2P[a\_1\ u + a\_2\ v + a\_3\ w, b\_1\ u + b\_2\ v + b\_3\ w, 
            c\_1\ u + c\_2\ v + c\_3\ w, u + I\ v, u - I\ v])\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell["It is better to factor the coefficients.", "Text"],

Cell["Map[Factor, %, {2}]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since we want a non-degererate conic, the first three points should not be \
at infinity.  This means that the product  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_3\ b\_3\ c\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is not zero.  The rest of the coefficient of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(y\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is just the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(3\[Times]3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " determinant made up of the three ",
  StyleBox["abc",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "s.  So, if the three points are ",
  StyleBox["not collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it is not zero.  But having ",
  StyleBox["equal ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["xy",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-coefficients and ",
  StyleBox["no",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\ y\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "-term is just what we expect of the formula for a circle, as can be seen \
from the next calculation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Tidy[\((x - r\ z)\)\^2 + \((y - s\ z)\)\^2 + t\ z\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In the above, by adjusting ",
  StyleBox["rst",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " the coefficients can be made arbitrary.  So ",
  StyleBox["any such polynomial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " represents a ",
  StyleBox["circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  And clearly any such polynomial passes through the two points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u \[PlusMinus] I\ y\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  These \"circles\" can have imaginary radii or centers, so you cannot \
always see them."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 5.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Let us calculate an ",
  StyleBox["envelope",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["dual conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(P2L[x, y, z, x + y + z, x + 2\ y + 3\ z]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In order to find out what ",
  StyleBox["point conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " this is the envelope to, we have to dualize the variables:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Dual[Envel[Dual[%]]]

Envel[%]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["The check shows we have the right answer.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 6.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  We want to show that if two points move with (different) ",
  StyleBox["uniform",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["velocities",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " along two distinct lines, then the line joining them moves as a ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " around a conic."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We begin by setting up the points on the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis and on the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis (those could be any two lines), and we may use a unit velocity on \
one line."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
ClearAll[f,g,h,a,b,c,p,q,r,line,ff,gg,t];
p = w + t u;
q = w + a(t+b)v;
line = Tidy[PE[p,q]] \
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
So the result is a parameterized moving line.  We make a table of \
several of these lines and then compute the dual conic determined by five of \
them.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
tt = Table[line,{t,-5,5}];
gg = Last[Factor[P2L@@tt[[Range[5]]]]]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Wow!  The result is quite simple!  What conic is the is the \
envelope?\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["ff = Dual[Envel[Dual[gg]]]", "Input"],

Cell["Ah, well, not as simple.  Let us look at some pictures.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(a = 2\/3; \)\), 
    \(\(b = 3; \)\), 
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[
      Join[{ff == 0}, \((#1 == 0&)\)/@tt] /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, \(-15\), 5}, {
        y, \(-3\), 10}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}]; \)\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Projective equivalence of conics", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Using tangents", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We will give two rather different proofs of the following theorem.  The \
basic idea is that generally it takes ",
  StyleBox["five",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "pieces of data to determine a conic uniquely."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  A non-degenerate conic is uniquely determined by ",
  StyleBox["three distinct points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the conic and the ",
  StyleBox["tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at  ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of them.  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["First proof:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the three points on the non-degenerate conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the ",
  StyleBox["intersection of the tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Since the tangents do not cut the conic at other points, it follows \
that among these four points, ",
  StyleBox["no three are collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, consider the five dual polynomials:\n\t\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(p\^2, q\^2, r\^2, p\ s, q\ s\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nIf we could show that they are ",
  StyleBox["linearly independent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then \[LongDash] by linear algebra and duality \[LongDash] there would \
be ",
  StyleBox["only one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (up to a scalar multiple) such that\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\^2] == PD[f, q\^2] == PD[f, r\^2] == \n\tPD[f, p\ s] == 
        PD[f, q\ s] == 0. \)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nBut this means exactly that the first three points are ",
  StyleBox["on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the conic and the fourth is ",
  StyleBox["on the two tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Suppose the five polynomials were ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " independent.   We would have a linear relationship:\n",
  StyleBox["\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{\(a\_1\ p\^2\), "+", 
            RowBox[{
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["a",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[\(q\^2\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["a",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[\(r\^2\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["a",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "4"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["p",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["s",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["a",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "5"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["q",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["s",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["0",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  StyleBox[",",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nwhere  not all the multipliers are zero.  This can be rewritten as:\n\t\t\
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{\(a\_1\ p\^2\), "+", 
            RowBox[{
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["a",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[\(q\^2\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "+", 
            RowBox[{
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["a",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"], " ", \(r\^2\)}], "+", 
            \(\((a\_4\ p + a\_5\ q)\)\ s\)}], "==", "0"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nLet ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(l == PE[p, q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Differentiating the above relationship by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we find:\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"2", " ", \(PD[l, r]\), " ", 
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["a",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"], " ", "r"}], "+", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox[\(PD[l, s]\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["(",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  RowBox[{
                    SubscriptBox[
                      StyleBox["a",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], "4"], 
                    StyleBox[" ",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox["p",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
                  StyleBox["+",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  RowBox[{
                    SubscriptBox[
                      StyleBox["a",
                        FontWeight->"Bold"], "5"], 
                    StyleBox[" ",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox["q",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], 
                StyleBox[")",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["0",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ",",
  StyleBox["\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "because",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["l",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              SuperscriptBox[
                StyleBox["p",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}], "]"}], " ", "==", " ", 
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            RowBox[{"l", ",", 
              SuperscriptBox[
                StyleBox["q",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"]}], "]"}], "==", 
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            RowBox[{"l", ",", 
              RowBox[{\(a\_4\ p\), "+", 
                RowBox[{
                  SubscriptBox[
                    StyleBox["a",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], "5"], " ", "q"}]}]}], "]"}], "==", 
          "0"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "But this would mean that the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are collinear, which is ",
  StyleBox["impossible",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Second proof:",
  StyleBox["  Let ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" be the three (distinct) points on the non-degenerate conic ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", and let  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" be the two tangents at ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[
  " respectively.  Since the tangents are distinct and do not further \
intersect the curve, the intersection ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" of the lines ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" is ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "not",
  StyleBox[" on the conic. Because ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[
  " is not on either tangent (being on the curve), it follows that ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "no three",
  StyleBox[" of ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" are ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "collinear",
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Let ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n == PE[p, q]\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", the line joining ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[
  ".  It is distinct from the two tangents.  From previous theorems we know \
that ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" represents the ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "same line",
  StyleBox[" as  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, s]\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[
  " , because it passes through the two points of tangency on the tangents \
from ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[".   But the line ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PE[r, s]\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["must cut the conic in two points (one of then being ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[") which harmonically sepatate ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["s",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" from where the line intersects ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[".  This point on the conic \[LongDash] call it ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["t",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" \[LongDash] is uniquely determined by given configuration",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\ {p, q, r, s}\),
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[".  But, among the four points ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["t",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", no three are collinear, which means that they are ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["-independent.  So, we have determined that ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["f ",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["must pass through all four points ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["t",
        FontSlant->"Plain"]]],
  StyleBox[
  ".  By duality, the vector space dimension of the homogeneous quadratic \
polynomials that do this is ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["2",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now consider linear combinations ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ l\ m + b\ n\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are scalars.  These are quadratic, and, since the product ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(l\ m\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is not a square, the two polynomials ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(l\ m\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\ n\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are lineraly independent.  Thus  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(l\ m\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\ n\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  span a ",
  StyleBox["2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-dimensional subspace.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By computation, using the rules for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["PD",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " we find:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["PD",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["[",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox[\(a\ l\ m\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["+",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["b",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox[" ",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], \(n\^2\)}]}], ",", "p"}], "]"}], "==", 
        "\n", "\t", 
        \(a\ PD[l, p] m\  + \ a\ PD[m, p] l\  + 2  b\ PD[n, p] n\), "\n", 
        "                                          ", "==", 
        \(a\ PD[m, p] l\)}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "because ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on both ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   Similarly,\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(PD[a\ l\ m + b\ n\^2, q] == a\ PD[l, q] m\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], " "}]]],
  ".  \nThis proves that both the lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["l",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are tangent to the conic  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ l\ m + b\ n\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Also, we find that for the point ",
  StyleBox["r",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " we have\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{"     ", 
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox["PD",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["[",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox[\(a\ l\ m\),
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["+",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox["b",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox[" ",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], \(n\^2\)}]}], ",", \(r\^2\)}], 
            "]"}], " ", "==", " ", 
          \(2  a\ PD[l, r]\ PD[m, r] + 2  b\ PD[n, r]\^2\)}]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".\nFor this to be zero, we have ",
  StyleBox["only one choice",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (up to a common factor), namely:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(a\  == \ \(-\ PD[n, r]\^2\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["b",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], \(PD[l, r]\ PD[m, r]\)}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".\nThis shows that in the ",
  StyleBox["2",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-dimensional family of conics has only ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " passing through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Since ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " does this, then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is uniquely determined and must represent the same curve as\n\t\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"-", 
            SuperscriptBox[
              RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
                RowBox[{"n", ",", 
                  StyleBox["r",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "]"}], "2"]}], 
          StyleBox[" ",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["l",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[" ",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["m",
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(PD[l, r]\ PD[m, r]\ n\^2\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".\nThus we have not only ",
  StyleBox["proved",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the theorem but also found a ",
  StyleBox["formula for the answer",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Here is an ",
  StyleBox["example",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". First we take two lines and two points on them."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[l, m, n, p, q, r, s, t]; \)\), 
    \(\(l = y - 2\ z; \)\), 
    \(\(p = u + 2\ v + w; \)\), 
    \(\(m = x - 3\ z; \)\), 
    \(\(q = 3\ u - v + w; \)\), 
    \({PD[l, p], PD[m, q]}\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Then we take a third point and make sure no three are collinear and \
the point is not on the first two lines.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(n = PE[p, q]; \)\), 
    \(\(r = \(-\(u\/2\)\) - 2\ v + w; \)\), 
    \(\((PD[#1, r]&)\)/@{l, m, n}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now we try to find the conic and then draw three tangents at the three \
points.  We also include the fourth point mentioned in the proof. Before \
showing the picture we relate ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["n",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, s]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by showing that they represent  the same line."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(s = PE[l, m]; \)\), 
    \(\(t = XP[s, PE[n, PE[s, r]], r, \(-1\)]; \)\), 
    \(\(f = \(-PD[n, r]\^2\)\ l\ m + PD[l, r]\ PD[m, r]\ n\^2; \)\), 
    \(Tidy[f]\n\), 
    \(Expand[PE[n, PD[f, s]]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Finally, here is the picture.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, n == 0, PE[s, r] == 0, PD[f, p] == 0, 
          PD[f, q] == 0, PD[f, r] == 0, PD[f, t] == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, 
        \(-13\), 13}, {y, \(-13\), 13}, \n\tAxes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
      PlotStyle \[Rule] 
        \(({Thickness[0.005], #1}&)\)/@{Red, Blue, Green, Blue, Blue, Black, 
            Black}]; \)\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Making projective transformations", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
The previous result on using tangents to determine a conic can be \
employed to prove an important conclusion.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["All",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " non-degenerate conics are ",
  StyleBox["projectively equivalent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and indeed two conics can be mapped one to the other so that three given \
distinct points on one are maped to three given distinct points of the other. \
   "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We already know that any four points, no three of which are collinear, can \
be projectively transformed in to any other such quadangle.  Hence, the data \
for one non-degenerate conic can be transformed into the data for any other \
such conic.  Because the data uniquely determine the conics, the projective \
transformation must map one conic to the other."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now we need to show that any two non-degenerate conics can be transformed \
into each other, and  moreover, the triples of points on the two conics can \
be chosen arbitrarily.  So, let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be one conic, and let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the other.  Both are assumed ",
  StyleBox["non degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Choose three points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on one conic and three points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(r\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the other.  In each case, no three are collinear.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(s\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the intersection of the tangents at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(s\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the intersection of the tangents at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(q\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   We then map the one quadrangle ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({p\_1, q\_1, r\_1, s\_1}\)]],
  " to the other ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p\_2, q\_2, r\_2, s\_2}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  But this transformation must carry the first conic over to the other.  \
",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "To check this approach in an example, we consider the ",
  StyleBox["standard hyperbola",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and a ",
  StyleBox["particular ellipse",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{x\ y == 1, x\^2\/4 + y\^2 == 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tAxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle \[Rule] \(({Thickness[0.005], #1}&)\)/@{Red, Cyan}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We then set up the two configurations of four points, three of \
which are on the respective conics.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, g, p, q, r, s, Subscript]; \nf = x\ y - z\^2; \np\_1 = u; \n
    q\_1 = u + v + w; \nr\_1 = v; \ns\_1 = PE[PD[f, p\_1], PD[f, q\_1]]; 
    \((PD[f, #1\^2]&)\)/@{p\_1, q\_1, r\_1, s\_1}\n\), 
    \(g = x\^2 + 4\ y\^2 - 4\ z\^2; \np\_2 = 2\ u + w; \n
    q\_2 = \(-2\)\ u + w; \nr\_2 = v + w; \n
    s\_2 = PE[PD[g, p\_2], PD[g, q\_2]]; 
    \((PD[g, #1\^2]&)\)/@{p\_2, q\_2, r\_2, s\_2}\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now we compute the matrix of the projective transformation that \
takes the first quadrangle to the second, and we apply the corresponding \
projective transformation to the hyperbola.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(M = 1\/64\ MQP[{p\_1, q\_1, r\_1, s\_1}, {p\_2, q\_2, r\_2, s\_2}]; \n\n
    M // MatrixForm\n\), 
    \(Factor[\(TL[M]\)[f]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Whew!  The desired ellipse is indeed the answer.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "There are many projective transformations that map the unit circle onto \
itself; for example, any  ",
  StyleBox["rotation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " about the origin will do that.  But are there others?"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Well, if the image curve is a circle, it must pass through the two \
circular points at infinity.  If we keep them fixed, and match a third choic \
of points we get the rotations:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, g, p, q, r, s, Subscript]; \nf = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \n
    p\_1 = u + I\ v; \nq\_1 = u - I\ v; \nr\_1 = u + w; \n
    s\_1 = PE[PD[f, p\_1], PD[f, q\_1]]; 
    \((PD[f, #1\^2]&)\)/@{p\_1, q\_1, r\_1, s\_1}\n\), 
    \(g = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \np\_2 = u + I\ v; \nq\_2 = u - I\ v; \n
    r\_2 = u - 2  Sqrt[2] v + 3  w; \ns\_2 = PE[PD[g, p\_2], PD[g, q\_2]]; 
    \((PD[g, #1\^2]&)\)/@{p\_2, q\_2, r\_2, s\_2}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(M = \ MQP[{p\_1, q\_1, r\_1, s\_1}, {p\_2, q\_2, r\_2, s\_2}]; \n\n
    M // MatrixForm\n\), 
    \(\(TL[M]\)[f]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
If we switch the circular points, we get a rotation composed with a \
flip:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, g, p, q, r, s, Subscript]; \nf = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \n
    p\_1 = u + I\ v; \nq\_1 = u - I\ v; \nr\_1 = u + w; \n
    s\_1 = PE[PD[f, p\_1], PD[f, q\_1]]; 
    \((PD[f, #1\^2]&)\)/@{p\_1, q\_1, r\_1, s\_1}\n\), 
    \(g = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \np\_2 = u - I\ v; \nq\_2 = u + I\ v; \n
    r\_2 = u - 2  Sqrt[2] v + 3  w; \ns\_2 = PE[PD[g, p\_2], PD[g, q\_2]]; 
    \((PD[g, #1\^2]&)\)/@{p\_2, q\_2, r\_2, s\_2}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(M = \ MQP[{p\_1, q\_1, r\_1, s\_1}, {p\_2, q\_2, r\_2, s\_2}]; \n\n
    M // MatrixForm\n\), 
    \(\(TL[M]\)[f]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
If we mix up the circular points with other points on the circle, \
we get a complex matrix.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, g, p, q, r, s, Subscript]; \nf = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \n
    p\_1 = u + I\ v; \nq\_1 = u - I\ v; \nr\_1 = u + w; \n
    s\_1 = PE[PD[f, p\_1], PD[f, q\_1]]; 
    \((PD[f, #1\^2]&)\)/@{p\_1, q\_1, r\_1, s\_1}\n\), 
    \(g = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \nr\_2 = u + I\ v; \nq\_2 = u - I\ v; \n
    p\_2 = u + w; \ns\_2 = PE[PD[g, p\_2], PD[g, q\_2]]; 
    \((PD[g, #1\^2]&)\)/@{p\_2, q\_2, r\_2, s\_2}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(M = 2\ MQP[{p\_1, q\_1, r\_1, s\_1}, {p\_2, q\_2, r\_2, s\_2}]; \n\n
    M // MatrixForm\n\), 
    \(\(TL[M]\)[f]\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
